Q&A on Double Ninth Festival|How to view US January Non-farm Payroll data unexpectedly high and future trend? - Economy in Review - Professional Financial News Website
Q: How do you assess the US non-farm payroll growth of 1 January, which surpassed expectations and what are your views on future trends?
A: On February 3rd, the US Labor Department released its January non-farm payroll data for 2023, with an increase of 5.17 million new jobs, significantly higher than market expectations of 1.89 million; unemployment rate fell to 3.4%, marking the lowest level in nearly five decades; hourly wage growth was up 4.4% year-over-year and 0.3% month-over-month, showing some moderation.
When looked at in aggregate terms, this data contains significant one-time and technical factors that may have affected results such as teachers returning to work after strikes, corrections of survey statistics methodologies, updates on seasonal adjustment factors, and revisions (reference January 2022’s upward revision of 50.4 million net hires to 36.4 million) made to non-farm payrolls figures, which might result in further changes (possibly upward). Non-farm employment is likely not as strong or robust in real-world outcomes as it appears from these figures.
From an institutional perspective, job losses among high-tech companies remain but to a lower extent; whereas new job creation has accelerated in the service sector as leisure hotels and education/healthcare have shown strong recoveries. These data indicate that there is still a labor shortage in the services industry.
Combined with an improving service sector PMI and employment vacancies and labor shortages data, together with strong consumer credit data, we judge that the US services sector has received rapid repair after extreme weather conditions and holidays.
We believe that the US labor market’s demand-supply tension will not improve without going through a recession.
Currently, there is still a supply gap of over 5 million in US labor markets, with labor participation rates rising slowly, especially among those aged 25 to 54 who have already reached their pre-pandemic peak.
Moreover, because of strong excess saving and consumption credit among US residents, the strength of consumer demand will further delay the impact on employment markets from tightening policies.
The tightness of employment in the US labor market will define the economy in 2023.
Adding to the near-end of the tightening cycle and with inflation expectations constantly fluctuating, we do not expect sustained declines in wage growth.
Before the latest data release, expectations for US interest rate hikes were overly optimistic in stock markets, bond yields, and exchange rates.
With inflation data continuing to improve, the Fed is shortening its tightening pace while also sustaining a fragile balance of market’s expectations for an overheating economy. But the market has been largely unimpressed with statements suggesting a more hawkish stance from the Fed – not just that interest rates will be reduced at their next move, but two cuts before year-end.
In an environment of labor market tightness and unclear tightening policy impact, we believe that the Fed’s guidance for an end-of-year rate hike is reasonable; extreme expectations in the market and crowded trading may cause some harm to the market.
Even so, the stubborn nature of wage growth will hinder the rapid decline in inflation that has been underway. As M2 data turned negative and leading indicators for various CPI items have gradually improved, US consumer price index (CPI) data have turned to a phase of rapid decline since October. The stubbornness of wage growth may provide some support for future inflation data; however, current market expectations cannot be refuted. After the next inflation data is released, it’s possible that markets could engage in a new round of speculative trading.
Book for Quickly Understanding Internet Finance
This book is very shallow; it can be considered an introductory course that will help you quickly understand the broad picture of internet finance but to really delve deeper, you need to follow the map and dig on your own.
Yet the time for this book was relatively earlier; the subsidy war between Alipay and WeChat Pay that has now cultivated user habits seems to be something the book did not mention. So, how small is it in terms of the current share of third-party payments?
With people traveling these days, they bring their phones to eat out instead of carrying cash; moreover, banks refuse to provide the high transaction volume that the AC/CRS system requires for payment, which has led some merchants to abandon the UnionPay card.
But still, I am very grateful to the author for writing this introductory book; it is evident that the author has had many years of financial observation experience and is familiar with both domestic and international financial development histories, which allows him/her to write in such an easy-to-understand manner.
Writing something large is easy; writing it in plain language and making it understandable requires skill.
