It is not recommended to read very highly
A while ago I read and just happened to see something scribbled from memory about some reflections on my reading.
This is more of an anthology of love fragments from everyday life than a collection of short stories.
The tone of this book is extremely mild and mundane – like drinking plain water – to an extreme level of brevity and economy in choosing words and sentences, as well as in constructing the plot, which resembles a quiet street on a summer afternoon.
Each short story is a tiny snapshot of time in everyday life, without beginning or end.
Finally gathered together, they were dubbed "When We Talk About Love, What Are We Talking About?".
I think this collection of short stories and its title represent an act of artistry; the author wants to convey that love is not merely the ups and downs on stage or the eternal vows in urban legends, nor is it just the passionate embrace between lovers. Love should also be the mundane life without passion, the uninteresting days repeating day after day, with no worries about living up to expectations, avoiding reality, and being lost in uncertainty about the future.
As you read the book, images of quarreling spouses next to you, uninteresting marriages known to you, and unsatisfactory relationships might come to mind.
This is precisely why I do not recommend this book: from an aesthetic standpoint, the style and plot of the novel are unremarkable; unless your life is like raging waters or as sweet as living in a candy castle, you will find yourself feeling bored when you read it.
And in terms of depth of thought, everyday situations do not surprise or expand one’s mind and perspective (this is unlike in Gabriel Garcia Marquez’s Love in the Time of Cholera), while brief episodes fail to address any issues, such as what kind of mistake led to the failure of love?
What causes the breakdown of the protagonist's marriage?
So I am not going to recommend reading this book; whether or not you read it doesn’t make much difference.
Authors' ideas are conveyed more through skills and form than through story, which is one reason this book fails to provide an engaging reading experience.
